hey everyone this is kevin from  thechestwebsite.com and today we're  going to be going over the  italian bologna trap which starts out in  the italian opening which is e4  e5 knight f3 knight c6 and then bishop  to c4 one of the most common openings in  all of chess  and many times black is going to respond  with the geoco piano and that is  bishop to c5 now white has kind of two  common themes that they'll go with here  both of them center around playing pawn  to c3 at some point and then pushing  forward here on  d4 they could give up upon a material  first the evans gamut so after the  bishop takes  pawn c3 bishop comes back and they push  forward here on d4 but many players  don't want to give up that pawn in the  evans gambit so they may just play  pawn to c3 first which is what the  italian bologna trap  is going to be using so after c3 black  has a couple options on there and they  could play d6  solidifying their pawn chain opening up  for the light square bishop to get  involved into the action maybe pinning  down the knight here on  f3 at some point or they could play  knight to f6 where  white's hey the whole reason you play c3  is to push forward here on d4  even if they take here you can push  forward put a lot of pressure  on their side of the board but in this  opening we're going to be looking at if  they play pawn  to d6 we're still pushing forward here  d4  after the exchange pawn takes here on  d4 then we're going to look at what  happens if black plays bishop here to  b4 which is a pretty logical move from  black standpoint  the bishop's under attack if it comes  back here to  b6 doesn't really feel like it's doing  too much but hey b4  this is there's no pawn to stop this  check here  so it looks like whites can either have  to play knight to c3  or even bishop to c2 to stop this check  now before we get into what white's best  move actually  is i do want to take a few steps back  and make sure that you  understand some concepts you should be  thinking about in a chess openings  one is this move c3 the the main thing  that you should be thinking about is  pushing forward here on d4 and the  geocopiano but another one is the fact  that this opens up for this queen to get  involved queen to b3  uh this is going to put a lot of  pressure on this f7 square with the  queen and the bishop eyeing down there  but also queen to a4 and as soon as  black plays  pawn to d6 it in any time  this tells me that this light square  diagonal right here  is open and so i need to be thinking  about  how can i at some point attack this  diagonal here  and attack the king here on e8  so later on i know that i can bring my  queen here to a4  and then if i can somehow attack this  knight if the knight's pinned already  then the knight's going to fall here but  if the knight's not there then my queen  could have a surprise attack against  other pieces so just keep that in mind  because that is going to come into play  in the baloney trap so after the  exchange right here bishop comes down to  b4 the best move from white really is to  play king 2f1 and yes this is how we  actually lay the trap  but it is also the best move and i'll go  over in just a minute  hey if we don't play f1 what happens if  we just play knight to c3 how does that  play out as well but we're going to be  looking at  king 2 f1 and then two options we are  going to look at from black  one is if they just develop materials so  they don't have  a attack on the king right away  this pawn here on d4 is protected by our  knight  and our queen so that the knight can't  come this bishop here on b4  can't do anything else and so they may  say let's get more material involved  into the game they could play  knight to f6 or they could play bishop  down here to  g4 so let's look at both of those  options the first one here on  knight to f6 and remember what we talked  about  is that this light square diagonal here  with the king at the end here on e8 is  vulnerable  and so we can start to chip away at this  one piece here  on c6 by playing pawn to d5  and once that knight moves since it's  under attack then white can play the  move queen  to a4 this is check and you can see this  bishop here on  b4 is going to fall and that  is essentially what the bologna trap is  to get that free  piece here on b4 now even if they  don't play knight to f6 you can still  have a very strong attack let's say they  played bishop down here to  g4 in this case we could play bishop or  queen to b3 and you can see this  is not only attacking the bishop it is  defended by the knight right now but we  can  play pawn to d5 at some point if we want  to but you can see it's also  threatening this square here on f7  and so black really has to deal with  that  and even if they take our knight right  here  uh it may be tempting to play something  like bishop to  f7 and then take with your queen over  here but i think you just take with your  pawn  now many times you may look at this and  say wow you know that that doesn't  really give  my king protection on the king side but  also you got to keep in mind that you're  not going to be castling on the king's  side  and so right now your rook is really  blocked in and there's no real  good way to do that you're also eyeing  down your material on the king side of  the board  and so by playing pawn takes here on f3  this opens up for your rook to come to  the semi-open  file here on g1 and have your rook  enter the attack alongside your light  square bishop your queen  and you can easily get your dark square  bishop involved as well  now if we come back we said we're gonna  look at what happens if we don't play  that king to f1 the main difference  let's say we play that knight to  c3 and then they play knight to f6 so  same situation as before but now we play  d5 yes the knight could just come to  let's say e5 as we said before and then  we can deploy  our queen to a4 but they have an  in-between move  and that is because the king is not on  f1  they can just exchange their bishop here  on c3  so after the pawn takes now that i can  come here to  a5 and now the threat is gone  even if the queen comes to a four check  well now just pawn to c6 this  knight here on a5 is protected by the  queen  the check has stopped from the queen  here and then black's going to be  looking to play pawn to  b5 with tempo here and gain control on  the queen side of the board  while black is or while white's really  trying to figure out okay how do i  continue the  attack so that is why if we come back  here i recommend not playing  the knight to c3 but instead playing the  king  2 f1 almost hoping that there's some  exchange right here so you can get your  rook over here to g1 but if they do  fall for the ultimate trap and that is  trying to get their  pieces involved into the game like  knight to f6 which makes a lot of sense  you have recognized that this light  square diagonal here is a huge weakness  you can remove that piece on this light  square diagonal  with pawn to d5 and after that rook the  knight moves  then the queen can come here to a four  check  and capture this bishop here on b4 so  that is the  italian bologna trap since i know many  of you do like to play the italian  opening i think you may have some  opportunity  to deploy this trap on your opponents  but thank you guys so much for watching  another trap video if you wouldn't mind  hitting that like button definitely  helps the channel out  a lot lets me know that you like these  videos but thanks so much for watching  and i'll see you guys in  the next video  you

